<!--
Volfied (World, rev 1) - Taito, 1989

=============================================================================
STATUS: THE RBF NOW EXISTS. THE ROM SET IS THE ONLY THING STILL MISSING.
=============================================================================

Volfied.sv (the emu wrapper), Volfied.qsf and Volfied.qpf were added on
2026-08-06 and the core compiles end to end: 0 errors, 31% of the ALMs, 39% of
the RAM blocks, releases/Volfied_YYYYMMDD.rbf produced. So the <rbf>Volfied</rbf>
tag below now names a file that exists.

It has NEVER BEEN RUN, because blocker B below still stands: without
cchip_c04-23 there is no .rom to load into it. Nothing beyond "it builds and
fits" is claimed.

Every ROM name, CRC, offset and DIP setting below is verified verbatim against
MAME's src/mame/taito/volfied.cpp and src/mame/taito/taitoipt.h. What has
changed since the previous revision of this file is which blockers are left.

CLEARED since the last revision:

  1. SOUND. The YM2203 (JT03) is vendored and rtl/ym2203_wrapper.sv exists.
     Volfied reads its DIPs through the chip's SSG ports A/B, which is why
     the two were one problem:
         ymsnd.port_a_read_callback().set_ioport("DSWA");
         ymsnd.port_b_read_callback().set_ioport("DSWB");

  2. THE BITMAP PLAYFIELD. rtl/volfied_bitmap.sv implements it against
     rtl/sdram.sv's new page-mode burst port: 512KB of 16bpp VRAM in SDRAM
     bank 1, the video_mask read-modify-write, video_ctrl page select, and
     refresh_pixel_layer()'s exact pixel decode. Validated in
     sim/tb_volfied_bitmap.sv against the real SDRAM chip model.

     NOTE the pixel format is 16 BITS PER PIXEL, not 8  - 
         "VIDEO RAM has 2 screens x 256 rows x 512 columns x 16 bits"
     and the format uses bits 15, 13, 12:9 and 8:6, so 8bpp cannot express it.

  3. ROTATION. ROT270 is handled by the framework's screen_rotate, which this
     core already ships and which KNOWN_ISSUES.md section 2 covers.

     ONE THING TO CONFIRM ON THE FIRST BOOT: the <rotation> tag below says
     "vertical (cw)", but volfied.cpp's GAME() line is ROT270, which is a
     COUNTER-clockwise quarter turn, and Volfied.sv's OSD therefore defaults
     to CCW. The tag drives nothing in the core - it is a frontend hint - so
     the two can disagree without breaking anything, but one of them is wrong
     and a single boot settles which.

  4. BOARD AND WRAPPER. rtl/volfied_top.sv assembles the 68000 map, the
     8192-entry palette, the 9-bit PC090OJ colour bank, the C-chip and the
     Z80 side; Volfied.sv wraps it for the framework. Both build.

  5. Z80 MAP. rtl/z80_bus_decoder.sv is parameterized on GAME_ID and carries
     Volfied's map: CIU at 0x8800/0x8801, RAM 0x8000-0x87FF (2KB, not 4KB - 
     the larger window would swallow the CIU), and a flat 32KB ROM with no
     bank window.

STILL BLOCKING:

  B. THE C-CHIP EPROM IS NOT IN YOUR ROM SET. See the next section. This is
     now the ONLY blocker, and it stops the .rom from building at all, before
     any RTL matters. Re-checked 2026-08-06 with tools/mra_build.py:
         MISSING FILE: 'cchip_c04-23' is not in any of the named zips

HIGH SCORES ARE NOW IMPLEMENTED. The <nvram> block below is fed by
rtl/volfied_hiscore.sv, which steals cycles on the 68000's work-RAM port to
read and write the table. Restoring happens automatically whenever this file
exists; saving is the momentary "SAVE HIGHSCORE" entry in the OSD, and the
core paints a message saying what happened. There is no autosave, because the
framework only ever collects a save request while its own menu is open.

=============================================================================
YOUR volfied.zip IS STILL MISSING THE C-CHIP EPROM  -  VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED
=============================================================================

Checked directly against roms/volfied.zip and roms/cchip.zip on 2026-08-06:

    volfied.zip  17 parts, all c04-*, no cchip_c04-23
    cchip.zip    cchip_upd78c11.bin only (4096 bytes)

and tools/mra_build.py reports:

    MISSING FILE: 'cchip_c04-23' is not in any of the named zips

That 8KB part is the C-chip's EXTERNAL game EPROM and it is per-game. The
4KB cchip_upd78c11.bin is the MCU's INTERNAL mask ROM and is the SAME
silicon in every C-chip board  -  Rainbow Islands, Volfied, Rastan alike. Only
the external EPROM is swapped per game, which is worth stating plainly
because it is a common and expensive thing to get backwards.

Refresh volfied.zip from a MAME 0.222-or-later set before expecting this
.mra to build.

=============================================================================
YOUR volfied.zip IS OUT OF DATE
=============================================================================

The C-chip EPROM `cchip_c04-23` referenced below is not present in the
volfied.zip in roms/. That zip predates MAME's C-chip dumps (added around
0.222) - it carries the 17 c04-* parts and the two unused PROMs, but no
cchip_c04-23. Refresh the set before expecting this .mra to build.

The 4KB internal mask ROM `cchip_upd78c11.bin` is a shared MAME device ROM and
lives in cchip.zip, exactly as for Rainbow Islands.

=============================================================================
STREAM LAYOUT
=============================================================================

Follows MAME's own region order, which is what any future Volfied support
would most naturally adopt. NOTE these offsets do NOT match the RGN_*
constants in rtl/rbisland_top.sv - those are Rainbow Islands' and would have
to be selected per GAME_ID (see rtl/game_config.svh).

    0x000000  68000 program + tile data  1024KB
    0x100000  Z80 program                  64KB
    0x110000  PC090OJ sprites             768KB
    0x1D0000  C chip ROMs                  12KB
                                        -
                                        0x1D3000

The two PROMs (c04-4-1.3, c04-5.75) are omitted: MAME marks that region
"// unused".
-->
<misterromdescription>
    <rbf>Volfied</rbf>
    <name>Volfied (World, rev 1)</name>
    <setname>volfied</setname>
    <year>1989</year>
    <manufacturer>Taito</manufacturer>
    <players>2</players>
    <joystick>4way</joystick>
    <rotation>vertical (cw)</rotation>
    <region>World</region>
    <mameversion>0.279</mameversion>

    <rom index="0" zip="volfied.zip|cchip.zip" type="merged" md5="none">

        <!-- 68000 program and tile data. MAME region "maincpu", 0x100000.
             Even offset takes the high byte of each word:
                 c04-12-1.30 @ 0x00000   c04-08-1.10 @ 0x00001
                 c04-11-1.29 @ 0x20000   c04-25-1.9  @ 0x20001
                 c04-20.7    @ 0x80000   c04-22.9    @ 0x80001
                 c04-19.6    @ 0xc0000   c04-21.8    @ 0xc0001 -->
        <interleave output="16">
            <part name="c04-12-1.30" crc="afb6a058" map="01"/>
            <part name="c04-08-1.10" crc="19f7e66b" map="10"/>
        </interleave>
        <interleave output="16">
            <part name="c04-11-1.29" crc="1aaf6e9b" map="01"/>
            <part name="c04-25-1.9"  crc="b39e04f9" map="10"/>
        </interleave>

        <!-- 0x40000..0x7ffff is a genuine hole in the region: main_map covers
             0x000000-0x03ffff and 0x080000-0x0fffff and nothing between.
             Filled with FF so every part after it keeps its MAME offset;
             FF is what unpopulated ROM space reads as on real hardware. -->
        <part repeat="0x40000">FF</part>

        <interleave output="16">
            <part name="c04-20.7" crc="0aea651f" map="01"/>
            <part name="c04-22.9" crc="f405d465" map="10"/>
        </interleave>
        <interleave output="16">
            <part name="c04-19.6" crc="231493ae" map="01"/>
            <part name="c04-21.8" crc="8598d38e" map="10"/>
        </interleave>

        <!-- Z80 sound program. One 32KB chip in a 64KB region; the upper half
             is not populated, padded to keep the following offsets fixed. -->
        <part name="c04-06.71" crc="b70106b2"/>
        <part repeat="0x8000">FF</part>

        <!-- PC090OJ sprites, 16x16. MAME region "pc090oj", 0xc0000.
             The last pair is ROM_RELOAD'd, i.e. the same 128KB appears twice,
             at 0x80000 and again at 0xa0000. That is not a mistake here - it
             mirrors the driver exactly. -->
        <interleave output="16">
            <part name="c04-16.2" crc="8c2476ef" map="01"/>
            <part name="c04-18.4" crc="7665212c" map="10"/>
        </interleave>
        <interleave output="16">
            <part name="c04-15.1" crc="7c50b978" map="01"/>
            <part name="c04-17.3" crc="c62fdeb8" map="10"/>
        </interleave>
        <interleave output="16">
            <part name="c04-10.15" crc="429b6b49" map="01"/>
            <part name="c04-09.14" crc="c78cf057" map="10"/>
        </interleave>
        <!-- ROM_RELOAD of the pair above -->
        <interleave output="16">
            <part name="c04-10.15" crc="429b6b49" map="01"/>
            <part name="c04-09.14" crc="c78cf057" map="10"/>
        </interleave>

        <!-- C chip: 4KB internal mask ROM then the 8KB game EPROM, same
             order as the Rainbow Islands .mra. -->
        <part name="cchip_upd78c11.bin" crc="43021521"/>
        <part name="cchip_c04-23" crc="46b0b479"/>
    </rom>


    <!-- HIGH SCORE NVRAM.
         From MAME's hiscore.dat, quoted verbatim:
             volfied:  @:maincpu,program,100200,26,00,50
         i.e. ONE block, 0x26 = 38 bytes at 0x100200, which is inside
         volfied.cpp's map(0x100000, 0x103fff).ram().

         size MUST equal `CFG_HS_TOTAL(`GAME_VOLFIED) in rtl/game_config.svh
         (38). rtl/volfied_hiscore.sv waits for the first byte to read 0x00
         and the last to read 0x50 before injecting, which is how it avoids
         restoring before the game has built its own table. -->
    <nvram index="2" size="38"/>

    <!-- DIP switches, resolved verbatim through MAME's Taito macros:
         DSWA = TAITO_MACHINE_COCKTAIL_LOC(SW1) + TAITO_COINAGE_WORLD_LOC(SW1)
         DSWB = the explicit block in INPUT_PORTS_START( volfied )

         Default byte values come from each PORT_DIPNAME's default argument:
             DSWA  bit0=0 bit1=1 bit2=1 bit3=1 bits5:4=11 bits7:6=11  -> 0xFE
             DSWB  bits1:0=11 bits3:2=11 bits5:4=11 bit6=1 bit7=0     -> 0x7F
         `ids` lists settings in ascending raw-value order, as MiSTer expects. -->
    <switches default="fe,7f" base="16">
        <dip name="Cabinet" bits="0" ids="Upright,Cocktail"/>
        <dip name="Flip Screen" bits="1" ids="On,Off"/>
        <dip name="Service Mode" bits="2" ids="On,Off"/>
        <dip name="Demo Sounds" bits="3" ids="Off,On"/>
        <dip name="Coin A" bits="4,5" ids="4C 1C,3C 1C,2C 1C,1C 1C"/>
        <dip name="Coin B" bits="6,7" ids="1C 6C,1C 4C,1C 3C,1C 2C"/>
        <dip name="Bonus Life" bits="8,9" ids="100k 500k,70k 280k 1400k,20k 40k 120k 480k 2400k,50k 150k 600k 3000k"/>
        <dip name="Difficulty" bits="10,11" ids="Hardest,Hard,Easy,Medium"/>
        <dip name="Lives" bits="12,13" ids="6,5,4,3"/>
        <dip name="32768 Lives (Cheat)" bits="14" ids="On,Off"/>
        <dip name="Language" bits="15" ids="English,Japanese"/>
    </switches>

    <!-- Volfied has exactly ONE button (IPT_BUTTON1 - the shield/laser) on a
         4-way stick. Pause is this core's own addition, not a PCB control. -->
    <buttons names="Shield,Start 1P,Start 2P,Coin,Service,Pause"
             default="A,Start,Select,R,L,X" count="6"/>
</misterromdescription>
